# Overview
#
# `biweekly` means both twice a week and every two weeks (`a biweekly
# release`), and `bimonthly`, `biannual`, and `biyearly` split the same
# way. The reader must guess the interval. The fix states the interval
# (`every two weeks`). The rule sits at Required, because it matches
# exact words, and each word names two intervals wherever it appears.
#
# Expression
#
# `main` lists each word in its one-word spelling. A space in a pattern
# is a whitespace token, so `bi weekly` in a pattern does not match
# `bi-weekly`. The `bi-` branch names the hyphen, so it matches the
# hyphenated spelling.
#
# Measurement
#
# The rule hit 3 lines in the measure.py corpus, and all 3 left the
# interval to the reader (`the bi-weekly SIG meetings`).
expr main [biweekly, bimonthly, biannual, biannually, biyearly, (bi-[weekly, monthly, annual, annually, yearly])]
let message "Ambiguous frequency. Write the interval, such as `twice a week` or `every two weeks`."
let description "Flags `biweekly`, `bimonthly`, `biannual`, and `biyearly`, which each name two intervals."
let becomes ""
let kind "Style"
allows "The report runs every two weeks."
allows "The binary runs weekly."
